few letters, so `\u00ed`, `\u00fa`, `\u00e7`, `\u00ea` and many others were never stripped. It now decomposes with Unicode NFKD and drops combining marks, so every accent is covered.\n2. **Mac vs. Windows text.** Macs often store `\u00fc` as `u` plus a combining mark, while Windows uses the single character `\u00fc`. The old code treated those as different names. NFKD decomposition makes both forms identical. It also turns non-breaking spaces into plain spaces before whitespace is collapsed.\n3. **`\u00df` vs `ss`.** `.lower()` leaves `\u00df` alone. I switched to `.casefold()`, which turns it into `ss`.\n\nI also changed `email_key` to NFKC normalization plus `casefold()`, so emails get the same Unicode handling. The first-seen record still survives.\n\nOne behaviour you might not expect: a record dropped as a duplicate doesn't register its own email. In my check, a later record that shared only that dropped record's email was kept. The old code did the same thing. Say so if you want dropped records' emails to count too.", "ttft_ms": 2127, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 15018, "uuid": "02362bd8-4a43-402f-87b3-3f5f4abfaea5", "ttft_stream_ms": 1211, "time_to_request_ms": 162, "first_content_frame_ms": 1211, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T17:51:13.856Z"}